after Euro 2022
. The SheGulls supporters=E2=80=99 club for Brighton Women said: =E2=80=9CN=
ow there are bigger and more passionate crowds with louder voices commandin=
g more respect and attention. Put two sets of them together, and you can ex=
pect issues as we see on occasions at the moment.=E2=80=9D

Under the policy, travelling fans wishing to sit with their own supporters =
can do so in a designated area, while others retain the choice of where the=
y want to sit. Some supporters believe separation is a foregone conclusion.=
 SheGulls added: =E2=80=9CIt would be nice to live in a world where it isn=
=E2=80=99t required, but the truth is it can be hard enough to generate an =
atmosphere at home from within our own fan base, never mind if they=E2=80=
=99re interspersed with Arsenal or Chelsea fans, for example. It feels inev=
itable.=E2=80=9D

Dedicated away seating has been welcomed by, among others, Manchester City =
Women Official Supporters Club (MCWFC OSC), who cite an =E2=80=9Cincrease i=
n Manchester City supporters at away matches=E2=80=9D as having =E2=80=9Cgi=
ven a real boost to the atmosphere in stadiums=E2=80=9D.

The FSA has taken a keen interest in developments and a spokesperson said: =
=E2=80=9CThe FSA and its members helped advocate for dedicated away section=
s, to help create community, atmosphere and allow travelling fans to connec=
t, and to have a visible travelling fan section for away players to look to=
wards.=E2=80=9D

The infrastructure is already in place to segregate fan bases in most stadi=
ums, where it is established for men=E2=80=99s games, but there is no stipu=
lated away-ticket allocation owing to varying stadium sizes. Manchester Uni=
ted Women=E2=80=99s Supporters Club (MUWSC) feels supporter segregation cou=
ld be a good move. =E2=80=9CThis doesn=E2=80=99t feel like a bad thing; seg=
regation often results in an increased atmosphere which players regularly c=
omment on as a positive thing,=E2=80=9D it said.

Some experiments have taken place, with the WSL-run League Cup final using =
=E2=80=9Cclub ends=E2=80=9D behind the goals with mixed seating in the othe=
r stands, a model also used at FA Cup finals and at the 2025 Euros.


Brighton fans at St Andrew=E2=80=99s watch their team take on Birmingham in=
 the WSL earlier this month. Photograph: Natalie Mincher/SPP/Shutterstock


The FSA added: =E2=80=9CIt is important now to look at whether home-only se=
ctions might be helpful for the same reasons: visibility, community and atm=
osphere. For us, this is about fan choice rather than =E2=80=98segregation=
=E2=80=99, and that choice is really important to an enjoyable matchday exp=
erience for all. It is important to consider general and neutral fans in th=
e conversation, too, and there may not be a one-size-fits-all answer for ev=
ery club.=E2=80=9D

The current policies used in the league were developed through dialogue amo=
ng the league, its member clubs and fans, through the FSA. This collaborati=
on has enabled initiatives such as the Fan Choice trial and the Stadium Des=
ign Guidance. The belief is that the women=E2=80=99s game requires a distin=
ct approach as its fanbase grows.

Tottenham, for example, have embraced the mixed home and away seating forma=
t. The club is understood to view its flexibility and family-friendly envir=
onment as a way to maintain atmosphere. It is felt an inclusive environment=
 does not diminish the passion or noise levels of home supporters. However,=
 it is also understood that the club may consider trialling a more segregat=
ed model if it is proven to improve the overall product and help the game g=
row.


