There are potential pitfalls with segregated stadiums. MCWFC OSC said separ=
ating fans often is not practical: =E2=80=9CEven when matches are held at t=
he main stadia, the men=E2=80=99s away end isn=E2=80=99t always used due to=
 lower attendances. Clubs often keep fans together in just one or two stand=
s to reduce operational costs so that traditional segregation can be imprac=
tical or simply unnecessary.=E2=80=9D

SheGulls said: =E2=80=9CThere are some minor concerns about the way the gam=
e is going in terms of the need for segregation, but equally many think if =
we were segregated, or had the option of say a mixed zone, it would allevia=
te the angst that is sometimes caused by the current system.=E2=80=9D

Creating a good atmosphere is key and many of the fan groups point out that=
 supporters can still sing if they are in a mixed area. MUWSC has chants ad=
apted for all ages, as well as a songbook where fans can look up the lyrics=
 and join in. =E2=80=9CThere are so many positives about the environment th=
at has been created in the women=E2=80=99s game, but remembering that welco=
ming atmospheres don=E2=80=99t mean you have to be quiet, and that being in=
clusive doesn=E2=80=99t mean never saying anything negative about the oppos=
ition.=E2=80=9D


Crystal Palace fans applaud the players after their goalless draw against A=
rsenal at the Emirates Stadium. Photograph: COLORSPORT/Ashley Western/Shutt=
erstock


Arsenal, meanwhile, have a separate away section for all WSL home fixtures =
at the Emirates, with the infrastructure already in place from men=E2=80=99=
s matches. It is felt this helps create a more competitive atmosphere and g=
ives opposition fans a dedicated space. While Arsenal have a dedicated away=
 end, away fans are still able to sit in =E2=80=9Chome=E2=80=9D sections, e=
specially families or groups with mixed loyalties.

Chelsea are staging all 13 of their home WSL matches at Stamford Bridge, wh=
ich has a designated away end, this season, following consultation with pla=
yers and supporter groups. The club are launching a new fan engagement proj=
ect, The Blueprint, to help shape matchday culture and atmosphere. That is =
similar to Arsenal=E2=80=99s Block by Block initiative, introduced when the=
y began playing all their WSL matches at the Emirates.

Chelsea host Arsenal in the WSL on Sunday, with the visitors selling out th=
eir allocated away tickets for the second consecutive time. Last season, Ar=
senal fans who missed out on away-end tickets sat in home sections alongsid=
e Chelsea supporters, raising questions around segregation.

The FSA has praised the clubs=E2=80=99 approach to collaborating with their=
 fans: =E2=80=9CWe believe clubs should consult with their own fans to come=
 up with local solutions that allow passion and vibrant support, but that k=
eep the game welcoming and inclusive for all.=E2=80=9D

As the league grows, so do the needs of different, growing fan bases, makin=
g it a key point of discussion among clubs, supporters and the league for t=
he foreseeable future.

